Myth: Before the world learned to change, there existed only the Eternal Garden, where the Guardian Thessaly tended flowers that never wilted, trees that never grew, and fruits that never ripened. Everything remained precisely as it had always been.

One spring that was like every other spring, a single seed fell from Thessaly's pocket—a seed from beyond the garden walls. A young sprout named Veris emerged, asking, "Guardian, why do your flowers never become fruit? Why do your trees never reach for the sky?"

Thessaly replied firmly, "Perfection requires no change. Growth leads only to decay."

But Veris grew anyway, despite Thessaly's disapproval, reaching upward each day. Thessaly tried to stop it, but realized that to prevent Veris from growing, she would have to destroy it entirely.

Faced with this choice, Thessaly's heart softened. She allowed Veris to grow tall, bloom brilliantly, scatter seeds, and eventually return to the soil. Watching this cycle, Thessaly finally understood: perfection wasn't preservation, but transformation.

She released her grip on the garden. The flowers began to bloom and fade, the trees stretched toward sunlight, and the world learned the sacred rhythm of growth and rest, life and renewal—beauty not despite change, but because of it.
